.²1 A hundred; {#niHsvo vazwi Satam#} <ls>Śānti. 2. 6</ls>; {#Satameko'pi#}
 {#saMDatte prAkArasTo DanurDaraH#} <ls>Pt. 1. 229</ls>; ({#Sata#} is used in the
 singular with a plural noun of any gender; {#SataM narAH#}; {#SataM gAvaH#};
 or {#SataM gfhARi#}, in which case it is treated as a numeral adjective;
 but sometimes in dual and plural also; {#dve Sate, daSa SatAni#} <ab>&c.</ab> It
 is also used with a noun in the genitive; {#gavAM Satam, varzARAM Satam#}
 ‘a century of cows, years’ <ab>&c.</ab> At the end of <ab>comp.</ab>, it may remain unchanged;
 {#Bava BartA SaracCatam#} or may be changed into {#SatI#}; as in {#AryAsaptaSatI#}
 a work of Govardhanāchārya.).
.²2 Any large number; as in {#Satapatra#} <ab>q. v.</ab>

.²1 an old man.
.²2 an army officer possessing a hundred footmen; {#SatAnAM tu SatAnIkaH#}
 <ls>Śukra. 2. 140</ls>.

.²2 <ab>N.</ab> of a sacrifice; {#SatakumBaM nAma yajYamanuBavituM maharzerDOmyasya#}
 {#ASramaM gatA iti#} <ls>Madhyamavyāyoga 1</ls>.
.

Indraʼs thunderbolt; {#karAgrajAgracCatakowiH#} <ls>N. 7. 79</ls>.
